o be needed for a chow or pung, they can keep more options open.\n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, for example, B4 has 3 left. The user has one B4. If they discard B4, maybe someone else could use it. But the user's hand already has a B4. Alternatively, if they discard W1, they have two, so discarding one would leave them with one W1. That could be useful if they want to form a pair later.\n\nWait, the user's hand is currently 13 tiles. To win, they need the 14th tile. Since the melds are empty, all the melds must be concealed. So the user is aiming for a fully concealed hand (4 points) plus other patterns.\n\nLooking at the tiles, the user has W1 (2), which is a terminal tile. W6 is a middle tile, W9 is another terminal. T2 and T4 are middle and middle, B2, B3, B4, B8 (B2-B4 is a sequence, B8 is a terminal in dots). F3, F4 are winds, J1 and J3 are dragons.\n\nThe hand has a mix of terminals and simples. The user might be building a hand with a mix of chows and pungs. But since the melds are empty, they need to form them by drawing or self-draw.\n\nIf the user discards a tile that's part of a potential chow, they might limit their options. For example, if they discard T2, they can't form a T1-T2-T3 chow. But if they discard W6, maybe they can still form a chow with W6-W7-W8 if they draw W7 or W8. However, the previous player has a W2-W3-W4 chow, so W5 might be available.\n\nAlternatively, the user could be aiming for an Outside Hand (4 points) which requires terminals and honors in every meld and pair. But since the hand has some middle tiles (W6, T4, B3, B4), maybe not. Unless they can form chows that end with terminals.\n\nAnother thought: the user has two W1s. If they keep one W1 as a pair, the rest could be chows. For example, W1 pair, and then maybe W6-W7-W8 (if they draw W7 and W8), but the user has W6. They also have B2-B3-B4, which is a chow. T2 could be part of a T2-T3-T4 chow if they get T3 and T4. Wait, the user has T2 and T4, but no T3. So if they keep T2