ck the unshown counts. J3 has 2 left. If I discard one, there are still others. \n\nIf I discard T7, which is a single tile, maybe others have it. The unshown T7 has 4 left. \n\nSo discarding T7 might be safer. \n\nThus, the best action is Play T7.\n</think>\n\nEvaluation: Discarding T7 preserves key sequences in T and B suits while allowing flexibility to form additional chows if needed. Keeping T2-T3-T4 and B2-B3-B4 intact supports potential All Chows or Mixed Straight patterns. J3 and J1 can serve as a pair or contribute to scoring patterns like Half Flush.  \nReason: T7 is a non-essential single tile not part of any sequence. Discarding it maintains critical sequences for All Chows (24 points) and opens opportunities for drawing W5/W8 or B5/B8 to complete additional chows. The concealed hand also qualifies for Fully Concealed Hand (4 points).  \nAnswer: Play T7"}
