 of suits. Maybe it's better to discard a tile that's not part of a potential chow or pung. For example, J2 or J3. Since I have two J3s, maybe discarding one J3 would be safe. Let's see, if I discard J3, there are 2 left unshown. Others might have some, but not sure. Discarding J3 might not help others much, as they would need two more for a pung.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard a suited tile that's part of a chow, like T3, but then I lose the chance to form that chow. However, if I discard a tile that's not part of a chow, like J2, then I can keep the chow possibilities.\n\nSo, between discarding J2, J3, or a suited tile. Let's think about the possible scoring patterns. If I can form a hand with all terminals and honors, that's 32 points. To do that, I need to discard the non-terminal suited tiles. The non-terminals in my hand are T2, T3, T4, B2, B3, B4. Terminals are W1 (1), W7 (7?), wait W7 is 7 in Characters? Wait, Characters (W) are 1-9, so W7 is a middle tile, not a terminal. Terminals are 1 and 9. So in my hand, the terminals are B9 (9 of Dots) and W1 (1 of Characters). The rest are middle tiles. So to get all terminals and honors, I need to discard T2, T3, T4, B2, B3, B4. That's a lot. But I can only discard one tile now. So maybe it's not feasible. So perhaps focusing on other patterns.\n\nAnother option: forming a mixed straight (123 in different suits, 456, 789). But I have some tiles that might fit. For example, W1 (1 of Characters), T2-4 (Bamboo 2-4), and maybe others. But I need more tiles. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, forming a mixed triple chow (three chows in different suits with same numbers). Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, forming a concealed hand with all chows. If I can keep the chows, like T2-3-4 and B2-3-4, that would be two chows. Then maybe others. But I need four chows and a pair. Let's see: T2-3-4 (Bamboo), B2-3-4 (Dots), and maybe others. Wait, I have W1, W7. Not sure. Maybe not enough.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard a tile that's not part of 